# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# xecret — environment variable reference
#
# THIS FILE DOCUMENTS VARIABLE NAMES ONLY. IT CONTAINS NO VALUES AND NEVER WILL.
#
# Real values live in Phase.dev. There is no .env file on any developer machine:
#
#     phase run -- npm run dev                        local development
#     phase run -- npm run db:migrate                 migrations
#     phase run -- sh scripts/deploy-web.sh <env>     build and deploy
#
# The deploy is a script and not a bare `wrangler deploy`, because a deployment
# is a build plus an upload and the two have to agree about which environment
# they are: only the script reads wrangler.toml for the environment being
# deployed and puts XECRET_PUBLIC_URL where `next build` can see it. See that
# variable below, and scripts/deploy-web.sh for the rest.
#
# See docs/adr/0002-root-key-custody.md for why the runtime never fetches
# secrets over the network.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── Database (ADR 0006) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ONE database, TWO logins. Both strings point at the same Neon database and
# differ only in the user. Setup walkthrough: docs/operations/database-setup.md

# The application's login. A restricted role with no DDL rights, and no UPDATE
# or DELETE on audit_logs — so a SQL-injection bug or a stolen connection string
# can neither drop a table nor erase the record of having tried.
#
# NOT the `xecret_app_permissions` role from migration 0002 — that one is NOLOGIN and cannot
# be connected to. It is a group role holding the privileges; you create a LOGIN
# role that is a member of it (e.g. `xecret_app_login`) and use that here.
#
# In a deployed Worker this is unused: the Hyperdrive binding supplies the
# connection string. This variable is for local development.
DATABASE_URL=

# The owner login, used by `npm run db:migrate` and by nothing else. It can
# create and drop tables, which is exactly why the application never uses it.
# Use Neon's DIRECT endpoint here, not the pooled one — migrations run DDL.
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=


# ── Encryption (ADR 0002) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Root Key Encryption Keys, as a JSON object mapping version to a base64url
# 256-bit key:
#
#     {"1":"<43 base64url characters>"}
#
# A map rather than a single value because rotation needs both keys readable at
# once: rows wrapped under version 1 stay readable until every one has been
# re-wrapped under version 2. Only when the re-wrap completes is version 1
# removed — and only then may its escrow shares be destroyed.
#
# ⚠  LOSING THIS PERMANENTLY DESTROYS EVERY CUSTOMER'S SECRETS. A database
#    backup does not help — ciphertext without the key is noise.
#    Generate it only via the ceremony in docs/security/key-recovery.md:
#    `npm run keygen` prints the exact JSON to paste, plus the escrow shares.
#
# In production this is NOT read from an env var: it is bound to the Worker from
# Cloudflare Secrets Store under the same name. See apps/web/wrangler.toml.
XECRET_ROOT_KEYS=

# Which Root KEK version new wrap operations should use. Increment on rotation,
# and only after the new version is present in XECRET_ROOT_KEYS.
XECRET_ROOT_KEY_VERSION=1


# ── Authentication: WorkOS AuthKit ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# See docs/adr/0010-workos-as-identity-provider.md. AuthKit's hosted pages do
# the signing in — email and password, Google, MFA, enterprise SSO — and the
# browser only ever *navigates* there: our server redirects to AuthKit and
# AuthKit redirects back. Nothing about WorkOS is in the client bundle, so none
# of these is a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable and none is needed by `next build`.
# WorkOS is asked who a person is exactly once per sign-in; xecret then issues
# its own session, and the tokens WorkOS returns are discarded.
#
# AuthKit is free to 1,000,000 monthly active users, so ordinary sign-in costs
# nothing per customer. What is NOT free: an Enterprise SSO connection or a
# Directory Sync connection. WorkOS bills $125 per month for each one from the
# moment it exists, used or not, which is why they are sold as add-ons rather
# than bundled into a tier. Staging connections are free and are where they get
# built. See .local/plans/v2/02-auth-sso.md.
#
# Staging and Production share nothing: separate keys, separate client ids,
# separate redirect URIs, separate Google credentials, separate user pools.
# Local development uses Staging. The key and the client id must come from the
# same environment, or every sign-in fails in a way that reads like a code bug.

# `client_…`. Not a secret — it is in every authorization URL a browser is sent
# to — so the deployed Worker takes it from apps/web/wrangler.toml, stated per
# environment. Set it here too, to the same value: this is what
# `npm run check:env` reads, and what `next dev` falls back to if wrangler.toml
# names none.
WORKOS_CLIENT_ID=

# `sk_…`, from the same WorkOS environment as the client id above. The format
# does not say which environment issued a key — WorkOS has used `sk_test_…`,
# `sk_live_…` and plain `sk_…` — so check the pairing in the WorkOS dashboard
# rather than by prefix. A credential: it authenticates every call to WorkOS,
# including the code exchange that decides who a sign-in is. Lives in Phase.dev
# and reaches a deployed Worker as an encrypted secret
# (`wrangler secret put WORKOS_API_KEY --env <env>`), never in wrangler.toml.
# Server-side only.
WORKOS_API_KEY=

# 32+ random characters: `openssl rand -base64 32`. The key the short-lived
# sign-in `state` cookie is MACed with — the cookie that ties the AuthKit
# callback to the browser that started the sign-in (the OAuth-CSRF defence) and
# carries the PKCE verifier. A credential: whoever holds it can complete a
# sign-in in somebody else's browser. Phase.dev → `wrangler secret put
# WORKOS_COOKIE_PASSWORD --env <env>`, like the key above, and independent of
# it — never derived from or equal to WORKOS_API_KEY.
#
# It does NOT seal a session: xecret keeps its own opaque, database-backed
# session cookie exactly as before, and nothing WorkOS issues — no access or
# refresh token — is stored in a cookie or anywhere else. The name is WorkOS's
# convention, not a description of what it does here.
WORKOS_COOKIE_PASSWORD=

# Note — no variable belongs here: there is deliberately no WORKOS_REDIRECT_URI.
# The callback URL is derived at runtime as `XECRET_PUBLIC_URL +
# /api/auth/callback`, so it cannot drift from the deployment's own origin — a
# redirect URI that disagrees with it fails at the one moment nobody is
# watching, on somebody else's login. Register that exact URL in the WorkOS
# dashboard for each environment (http://localhost:3030/api/auth/callback in
# Staging, for local development).


# ── Application ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Public origin. Used for CLI callback validation, cookie scope, and OAuth
# redirect registration. Must exactly match the deployed domain.
#
# Read twice, at two different times, and both matter. The Worker reads it at
# runtime from the binding declared in apps/web/wrangler.toml. `next build`
# reads it from *this* environment, because every documentation page is
# prerendered and its canonical URL, sitemap entry and robots.txt Host line are
# decided before a binding exists. scripts/deploy-web.sh keeps the two in step
# by exporting wrangler.toml's value for the environment being deployed; a
# build run by hand has to set it, and refuses to guess when XECRET_ENV names a
# deployment.
XECRET_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3030

# development | staging | production
XECRET_ENV=development


# ── Email (ZeptoMail) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# xecret sends exactly one kind of mail: the link that invites somebody to an
# organisation. Everything else in the product works without this, so leaving
# these blank is a supported configuration — the invitation flow then reports
# that email is not configured rather than silently failing. Vault recovery
# needs no mail at all: recovery codes are held by the user, and nobody at
# xecret can issue a replacement.
#
# Where each value comes from, in the ZeptoMail console (zoho.com/zeptomail):
#
#   1. Add and verify your sending domain under Mail Agents → Domains. Zoho
#      refuses a send from an unverified domain rather than rewriting it, so
#      this step is not optional.
#   2. Create a Mail Agent, then open its "Setup" → "API" tab.
#   3. Copy the Send Mail Token. The console shows it as
#      `Zoho-enczapikey wSsVR…` — pasting the whole line is fine, the prefix is
#      stripped on the way in.
#
# This is a credential: it authorises sending mail as your domain. It belongs in
# Phase.dev / Secrets Store, never in a committed file.
ZEPTOMAIL_TOKEN=

# Must be an address on the verified domain.
ZEPTOMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=noreply@yourdomain.com

# The display name beside that address. Defaults to "xecret" when blank.
ZEPTOMAIL_FROM_NAME=xecret

# The regional API host. A token is only valid in the data centre that issued
# it, so an account created outside the default region MUST set this — otherwise
# every send fails authentication with a 401 that says nothing about why.
#
#   default / US   https://api.zeptomail.com/v1.1/email   (leave blank)
#   Europe         https://api.zeptomail.eu/v1.1/email
#   India          https://api.zeptomail.in/v1.1/email
#   Australia      https://api.zeptomail.com.au/v1.1/email
#   Canada         https://api.zeptomail.ca/v1.1/email
#   Japan          https://api.zeptomail.jp/v1.1/email
#   Saudi Arabia   https://api.zeptomail.sa/v1.1/email
ZEPTOMAIL_API_URL=


# ── Cloudflare (deploy time only) ────────────────────────────────────────────

CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# ── Firebase password migration (P14, one-off) ───────────────────────────────
#
# The scrypt parameters Firebase used to hash existing passwords. They are what
# lets WorkOS verify an imported hash, so that nobody is asked to reset a
# password they already know — a mass "reset your password" email on a
# credentials product is indistinguishable from a phishing campaign, and would
# lose every user who correctly assumes it is one.
#
# Inputs to the WorkOS migrations CLI, not to code in this repository:
#   npx workos migrations export firebase --from-file users.json \
#     --signer-key <key> --salt-separator <sep> --rounds 8 --memory-cost 14
#
# Firebase console → Authentication → Users → ⋮ → Password hash parameters.
# The signer key is as sensitive as a password: it is what makes every stored
# hash verifiable. Delete these once the migration is complete and verified.
FIREBASE_SCRYPT_SIGNER_KEY=
FIREBASE_SCRYPT_SALT_SEPARATOR=
FIREBASE_SCRYPT_ROUNDS=
FIREBASE_SCRYPT_MEM_COST=
